    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Nausea, vomiting, and loose stools for two days during tuberculosis treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Holliday is a 21-year-old dentist with drug-susceptible cavitary pulmonary tuberculosis in the sixth week of the continuation phase of isoniazid and rifampin, given by video-observed therapy, and with a mild alcohol use disorder. Two days ago he developed nausea and loose stools, and he vomited twice yesterday; two coworkers at the dental office had a stomach illness last week. When he reported the nausea on his video call this morning, the health department nurse told him to hold today&apos;s doses, as his treatment plan instructs, and to be seen today with liver tests. He has no yellowing of the eyes or skin, no dark urine or pale stools, no pain under the right ribs, and no itching or rash. His cough has gone and he has had no fevers or night sweats. He has not missed any other dose. He told us that nine days ago, at a friend&apos;s birthday party, he drank about six whiskeys over an evening, the first time he has drunk heavily since starting treatment; he has not drunk since. He feels he let himself down but has no thoughts of suicide. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Upper abdomen and bowel</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Nausea, two vomits, and loose stools</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild to moderate; drinking fluids</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Sixth week of the continuation phase</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Coworkers with a stomach illness; one evening of heavy drinking nine days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Today&apos;s doses held on the nurse&apos;s advice</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No jaundice, dark urine, right upper abdominal pain, rash, or fever</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
