    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis (A08.4)</content>. A short illness shared with coworkers, with loose stools, no fever, and a benign abdomen. ALT 64 and AST 58 U/L are only just above normal, with normal bilirubin 0.9 and alkaline phosphatase 98. Drug-induced liver injury is defined as ALT at least three times the upper limit with symptoms, or five times without; ALT here is about 1.1 times the upper limit, so this is not drug-induced liver injury. The small rise fits the recent heavy drinking and the viral illness. [S]<list><item>Oral fluids and a bland diet; no antiemetic needed now.</item><item>Hand washing; stay off work until two days after the symptoms stop.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, continuation phase (A15.0)</content>. Clinically well on treatment, with no cough or fever. One day&apos;s doses held today. [S]<list><item>Resume isoniazid and rifampin tomorrow under video-observed therapy once he can keep food down; the missed dose is added at the end, so the last dose moves one day later.</item><item>Repeat liver tests in two weeks.</item><item>Stop the doses and call the same day for yellow eyes or skin, dark urine, right upper abdominal pain, or vomiting that returns.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, mild, with one lapse (F10.10)</content>. One evening of heavy drinking after more than two months with little or none. AUDIT-C 4. A lapse he has reported openly. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline is in his phone. [S]<list><item>Brief counseling today about the extra liver risk from alcohol on isoniazid; he recommits to no alcohol until treatment ends.</item><item>Plan for parties: arrive with a non-alcoholic drink and leave early, and tell one friend his goal.</item><item>Next session with his substance-use counselor this week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Health department nurse to confirm the restart on tomorrow&apos;s video call. Liver tests in two weeks. End-of-treatment visit in the summer. Next scheduled visit 1873-08-18: end-of-treatment review after six months. [S]</paragraph></text>

